  "evidence": [
    {
      "sourceId": "src-30908e698efb2f9d",
      "quote": "Developing Dialogic Education Theory, Methodology and Practice The Faculty of Education has a history of world-leading research on educational dialogue. CEDiR’s aim is to consolidate and extend this research, reaching across disciplines and contexts to develop the potential of educational dialogue at all education levels and to have an impact on theory, policy and practice. More about our aims and perspectives is summarised in the CEDiR Working Paper , collaboratively written by many of the group's members as a dialogue. CEDiR members represent a range of Faculty colleagues, drawn together through a common interest in dialogic education. The group seeks to promote collaboration and develop capacity at all levels, with a focus on supporting postgraduate students and early career staff. CEDiR is forging links with senior academics and practitioners in the field worldwide and already has a substantial number of high profile international collaborators and associates. Its main priority is on creating a supportive environment for creating and sharing high quality research that will shape the future of education. Please take a look at our upcoming CEDiR events . For queries email cedir @ educ.cam.ac.uk . CEDiR Research Strands CEDiR has several inter-connected Research Strands . These build on existing work undertaken by the Faculty of Education to consider important contemporary topics relating to dialogic education. Research undertaken includes every level of education from early years to HE, professional dialogues, and other dialogue outside of classrooms. Multimodal dialogue Dialogue, professional change and leadership Digital technology and dialogue Classroom dialogue and practitioner inquiry CEDiR Projects Examples of current/recent projects undertaken by CEDiR colleagues include: Talking Objects Toolkit for Systematic Educational Dialogue Analysis (T-SEDA) DIalogue and Argumentation for Cultural Literacy Learning in Schools (DIALLS) Digitalised Dialogues Across the Curriculum (DiDiAC) ED:TALK – Evidence & Dialogue Toolkit Thinking Together Virtual Internships Project (VIP) CEDiR Members The group is led by Sara Hennessy . The CEDiR steering group also includes Imogen Casebourne , Emma Cooper , Riikka Hofmann and Alison Twiner . PhD students Xinyue Zhou, Bill Bradley, Nunu Zheng and Stacy Marshall support CEDiR's communications and community activities. See the full list of members and our collaborators and associates . Subscribe to the termly CEDiR newsletter (Privacy Policy) : Internal mailing list: Colleagues and students based in Cambridge should email cedir @ educ.cam.ac.uk to sign up our internal mailing list for local events and news.",
